export const C6={title:'Admitting a patient (Skill V)',hook:'Coming into a hospital or nursing home can feel scary. The admitting caregiver is often the first friendly face a patient meets. Skill V has 16 steps and 10 minutes. You will put on an ID bracelet, check belongings and show the patient around.',objectives:['Explain why a warm admission helps patients feel safe.','Place an ID bracelet and handle paperwork and belongings.','Orient a patient to the room and the facility.','Finish with the call light, a safety check, reporting and documenting.'],sections:[
['Why admission matters',[
'**Admission** is when a patient first arrives to stay. The patient may be worried, in pain or confused. A calm, friendly caregiver helps the patient feel **safe** and **welcome**. The patient also learns how to get help, which keeps them safe. Family members may come too; greet them kindly and include them when the patient wants that.',
'During admission, the nurse collects health information. The caregiver helps by placing the **ID bracelet**, checking **belongings**, **orienting** the patient to the room and the building and often measuring **vital signs**, height and weight. Everything you see or hear stays **private**.',
chk('Why does a friendly admission matter?',['It helps the patient feel safe and know how to get help','It makes the stay shorter','It replaces the nurse’s check','It is not important'],'Feeling safe and knowing how to call for help prevents problems.')]],
['Starting the admission: steps 1–7',[
'**1.** Gather equipment and supplies, such as the ID bracelet, admission paperwork and a belongings list. **2.** Wash your hands or use hand rub. **3.** Greet the patient and introduce yourself. **4.** After you **identify the patient**, put the **identification bracelet on the patient’s wrist**. The bracelet helps every staff member know who the patient is, which prevents mistakes with care.',
'**5.** Explain what you are going to do. **6.** Give **privacy**—close the door or curtain. **7.** Get the **admission paperwork** from the patient and hand it to your **supervisor** (in the event, the judge plays the supervisor).',
order('Start of Skill V',['Gather supplies','Wash hands','Greet and introduce yourself','Identify the patient and place the ID bracelet','Explain the skill','Provide privacy','Hand the paperwork to the supervisor'])]],
['Belongings and valuables: step 8',[
'**8a.** Check that the patient’s **belongings are in the room**. **8b.** **Ask** whether the patient wants to put any **valuables**—money, jewelry, credit cards—in the **safe**. Many facilities make a written list of belongings so nothing gets lost. Glasses, hearing aids and dentures are very important; make sure the patient knows where they are kept.',
'Treat belongings with respect. Ask before you touch or move them. If something seems to be missing, tell the nurse right away.',
sort('Room or safe?',['Keep in the room','Offer to put in the safe'],[['Glasses',0],['A large amount of cash',1],['Slippers',0],['Expensive ring',1],['Hearing aids',0],['Credit cards',1]])]],
['Orientation to the room: step 9',[
'Show the patient how the room works. **9a.** Point out the items on the **bedside table** (such as the water pitcher and tissues). **9b.** Show how to use the **overbed table**. **9c.** Teach how to use the **call light**, **bed controls**, **TV** and **light controls**. **9d.** Explain how to make **telephone calls** and put the telephone **within reach**. **9e.** Show the **bathroom** and how to use the **bathroom emergency call light**.',
'Ask the patient to show you how to use the call light. This is called **teach-back**. If the patient can do it, you know they understood.',
match('Room orientation',[['Call light','Calls the staff for help'],['Bed controls','Raise or lower the head and foot of the bed'],['Overbed table','Holds meals and items over the bed'],['Bathroom call light','Calls for help from the bathroom'],['Telephone','Placed within reach'],['Bedside table','Water, tissues and personal items']])]],
['Orientation to the facility: step 10',[
'Next, explain the building. **10a.** Tell the patient the **visiting hours** and rules. **10b.** Explain how to find the **nurse’s station**, **chapel** and **dining room or cafeteria**. **10c.** Explain how to **tell who staff members are**, such as by their **uniform** or **ID badge**. **10d.** Explain **when meals are served** and how to **ask for snacks**.',
'Use simple words and do not rush. Older patients or people who are tired may need you to repeat things. Writing key information on a whiteboard in the room can help.',
flip('Facility facts to share',[['Visiting hours','When family and friends may visit'],['Nurse’s station','Where the nurses work'],['Staff ID','Uniform colors or ID badges'],['Meals','Meal times and how to ask for snacks'],['Chapel','A quiet place for prayer or reflection'],['Cafeteria','Where visitors can eat']])]],
['Finishing: steps 11–16',[
'**11.** Make sure the patient is **comfortable** and put the **call light within reach**. **12.** Do a **safety check** before leaving—bed low, wheels locked, path clear. **13.** Wash your hands. **14.** Use kind words and body language the whole time. **15.** **Report any concerns** to the nurse (judge), such as pain, confusion or missing items. **16.** **Document** that you admitted the patient.',
kase('Admission check',{a:{text:'The patient says, “I brought my grandmother’s gold necklace.” What should you do?',choices:[['Ask if they would like to put it in the safe','b','Correct. Offer the safe for valuables.',true],['Put it in your pocket to keep it safe','b','Never take a patient’s belongings yourself.',false]]},b:{text:'You finish the room tour. The patient seems unsure about the call light. What now?',choices:[['Show it again and ask the patient to try it','c','Correct. Teach-back checks understanding.',true],['Leave—they will figure it out','c','The call light keeps them safe.',false]]},c:{text:'You place the call light within reach, do a safety check, wash your hands, report and document. Admission complete!',end:true}})]],
["Height, weight and helping a new patient settle in",["During admission, caregivers often measure **height** and **weight**. The nurse uses them to plan care and to check medicine doses. Use the same scale each time, have the patient remove shoes and heavy items, and **balance or zero the scale** before weighing. Patients who cannot stand may be weighed on a chair or bed scale. Record the numbers right away so you do not forget them.","A new place can make anyone feel lost. Some patients may be **sad, angry or afraid**. Listen, be patient and answer questions you can answer; take other questions to the nurse. Respect each person’s **culture, religion and language**—ask how they would like to be addressed, and use an interpreter when needed rather than a family member. Small kindnesses, like putting family photos where the patient can see them, help a room feel like home.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Weighing on a standing scale","steps":["Identify the patient and explain","Balance or zero the scale","Patient removes shoes and heavy items","Patient stands still in the center of the scale","Read the weight","Help the patient off and record the weight"]},{"t":"check","q":"A patient speaks only Spanish and you do not. What is best?","options":["Ask the nurse for a trained interpreter","Speak louder in English","Skip the orientation","Ask another patient to translate"],"why":"Trained interpreters protect accuracy and privacy."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"What judges look for","text":"Judges check that you place the ID bracelet only after you identify the patient, that you hand the paperwork to the supervisor and that you ask about valuables instead of taking them. During orientation they listen for every item on the rating sheet: bedside table, overbed table, call light, bed controls, TV and lights, telephone, bathroom call light, visiting hours, locations in the building, staff identification and meals. Use a mental checklist in the same order every time so you do not skip one. End with the call light, the safety check, reporting and documenting."}]]
],takeaways:['Admission: gather supplies, wash hands, greet, identify and place the ID bracelet, explain, give privacy and hand paperwork to the supervisor.','Check belongings and offer the safe for valuables; orient to the room (call light, bed, TV, phone, bathroom call light) and the facility (visiting, locations, staff ID, meals).','Finish with comfort and the call light, a safety check, hand hygiene, reporting concerns and documenting.'],terms:[['Admission','When a patient first arrives to stay in a facility.'],['Orientation','Showing a patient how the room and building work.'],['Valuables','Expensive or important belongings such as money or jewelry.']],mcqs:[q('When do you place the ID bracelet?','After identifying the patient','Before greeting the patient','At the end of the skill','Only if the nurse asks','It prevents mistakes.'),q('Who gets the admission paperwork in the event?','The supervisor (judge)','The family','The patient keeps it','It is thrown away','Hand it to the supervisor.'),q('What should you ask about valuables?','Whether the patient wants them in the safe','Whether you can keep them','Nothing','How much they cost','Offer the safe.'),q('Which is part of room orientation?','How to use the call light','Visiting hours','Where the chapel is','When meals are served','The others are facility orientation.'),q('Which is part of facility orientation?','How to identify staff by uniform or badge','How to use the bed controls','Where the overbed table is','How to use the TV','Room versus facility.'),q('What is teach-back?','Asking the patient to show or tell you what they learned','Teaching the nurse','Going back to the classroom','Repeating yourself many times','It checks understanding.'),q('Where should the telephone be?','Within the patient’s reach','In the hallway','In a drawer','On the floor','So they can call family.'),q('What is the last step of Skill V?','Document the skill','Greet the patient','Place the ID bracelet','Explain the skill','Report, then document.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to admit a patient following HOSA Skill V.',rubric:[['Starting',['supplies','hand','greet','identify','bracelet','explain','privacy','paperwork']],['Belongings',['belongings','valuables','safe']],['Orientation',['call light','bed controls','overbed','telephone','bathroom','visiting','nurse','staff','meals']],['Finishing',['comfortable','call light','safety check','report','document']]],model:'I gather the supplies, wash my hands, greet the patient and introduce myself. After identifying the patient, I place the ID bracelet on their wrist, explain what I will do, provide privacy and hand the admission paperwork to my supervisor. I check that the patient’s belongings are in the room and ask if they would like to put any valuables in the safe. I orient them to the room by showing the items on the bedside table and how to use the overbed table, the call light, bed controls, TV and lights, the telephone, which I place within reach, and the bathroom and its emergency call light. I orient them to the facility by explaining visiting hours, how to find the nurse’s station, chapel and cafeteria, how to identify staff by uniform or ID badge and when meals are served and how to ask for snacks. Then I make sure the patient is comfortable with the call light within reach, do a safety check, wash my hands, report any concerns to the nurse and document the admission.'},sources:[RULES,CMS_RIGHTS,AHRQ_FALLS]};

export const C7={title:'Transferring a patient with a weak side (Skill VI)',hook:'Moving a patient from bed to a wheelchair is one of the most common caregiver tasks—and one of the riskiest. A fall can cause broken bones. Skill VI has 20 steps and 6 minutes. A gait belt, good body mechanics and the patient’s strong side keep everyone safe.',objectives:['Use good body mechanics to protect your back.','Set up the chair or wheelchair on the patient’s strong side.','Apply a gait belt correctly.','Perform a stand-pivot transfer following HOSA Skill VI.'],sections:[
['Body mechanics and fall safety',[
'**Body mechanics** means using your body safely when you lift or move. Keep your **feet apart** for a wide, steady base. **Bend your knees**, not your back. Keep the patient **close to your body**. **Turn with your feet** instead of twisting your waist. Tell the patient what you are doing and count together so you move at the same time.',
'Patients who are weak from a **stroke**, surgery or illness are at high risk of **falling**. The **strong side** must lead the move, because the patient can push and step with that side. **Non-slip slippers**, a **locked wheelchair** and a **gait belt** all help prevent falls. Watch for **dizziness** when the patient sits up—blood pressure can drop and make them faint.',
sort('Good or poor body mechanics?',['Good','Poor'],[['Feet about 12 inches apart',0],['Bending at the knees',0],['Twisting at the waist',1],['Holding the patient far from your body',1],['Turning with your feet',0],['Counting together before moving',0]])]],
['Setting up: steps 1–11',[
'**1.** Gather equipment: wheelchair or chair, gait belt, robe, non-slip slippers and a blanket. **2.** Wash your hands. **3.** Greet the patient and introduce yourself. **4.** Identify the patient. **5.** Explain the skill and provide privacy. **6.** Put the **wheelchair at the head of the bed facing the foot**, or at the **foot of the bed facing the head**. If the patient has a **weak side**, put the chair on the **strong side**.',
'**7.** **Lock the wheelchair wheels** and fold up the footrests. **8.** **Lower the bed and lock the bed wheels**. **9.** Raise the **head of the bed**. **10.** Lower the **side rail** on the exit side. **11.** **Fan-fold** the bed linens to the foot of the bed.',
order('Set up the transfer',['Gather equipment and wash hands','Greet, identify, explain and give privacy','Place the chair on the strong side','Lock wheelchair wheels and fold up footrests','Lower and lock the bed','Raise the head of the bed','Lower the exit-side rail and fan-fold linens'])]],
['Dangling and the gait belt: steps 12–14',[
'**12.** Help the patient sit on the edge of the bed with their feet hanging down. This is called **dangling**. Watch for **signs of stress**: dizziness, pale skin, sweating or trouble breathing. If you see these, help the patient lie back down and tell the nurse. **13.** Put on the patient’s **robe** and **slippers with non-slip soles**.',
'**14.** Put on the **gait belt**: **a.** around the patient’s **waist, on top of clothing**; **b.** with the **buckle in front**; **c.** tight enough that you can slide **your fingers under the belt**. A gait belt gives you a safe place to hold. Never lift a patient by the arms or under the armpits—it can hurt their shoulders.',
chk('How tight should the gait belt be?',['Snug, with room to slide your fingers under it','As tight as possible','Loose enough to slide down to the hips','It does not matter'],'Snug but not too tight; fingers fit under the belt.')]],
['The stand-pivot transfer: step 15',[
'**15a.** Stand **in front** of the patient with your **feet about 12 inches apart**. **15b.** **Brace** the patient’s knee with **one or both of your knees**. **15c.** Tell the patient to **push against the bed with their hands** to stand on a signal, such as “**on the count of three**.” **15d.** On the signal, help the patient stand, **lifting up on the belt** while the patient pushes up.',
'**15e.** Tell the patient to take **small steps** toward the chair. **15f.** Help them **pivot** until the backs of their legs touch the seat, then have them **place their hands on the armrests**, **bend their knees** and **sit down**. **15g.** Adjust the **footrests** and **remove the gait belt**.',
order('Stand, pivot, sit',['Stand in front, feet about 12 inches apart','Brace the patient’s knee with yours','Patient pushes on the bed; count to three','Lift up on the belt as the patient stands','Small steps toward the chair','Pivot until legs touch the seat','Hands on the armrests, bend knees and sit','Adjust footrests and remove the gait belt'])]],
['Finishing: steps 16–20',[
'**16.** **Cover** the patient with a blanket or sheet for warmth and privacy. **17.** Ask if the patient is **comfortable**. **18.** Wash your hands. **19.** Use kind words and body language. **20.** Use standard precautions the whole time.',
'Make sure the patient can reach the **call light** and anything they need, such as water or glasses, even though the rating sheet ends at step 20. Good caregivers always leave the patient able to call for help.',
flip('Transfer memory cards',[['Chair placement','On the strong side'],['Lock','Wheelchair wheels and bed wheels'],['Dangle','Watch for dizziness and stress'],['Gait belt','Waist, over clothes, buckle in front, fingers fit'],['Signal','On the count of three'],['Sit','Legs touch the seat, hands on armrests, bend knees']])]],
['Practice scenarios',[
kase('Transfer safety',{a:{text:'Your patient had a stroke and has a weak left side. Where do you put the wheelchair?',choices:[['On the patient’s right (strong) side','b','Correct. The strong side leads.',true],['On the left side','b','The weak side cannot support the move.',false]]},b:{text:'While dangling, the patient says, “I feel dizzy.” What do you do?',choices:[['Stay with the patient, help them lie back and tell the nurse','c','Correct. Dizziness is a sign of stress.',true],['Stand them up quickly before it gets worse','c','Standing could cause a fall.',false]]},c:{text:'Later the patient feels fine. You apply the gait belt, count to three and complete the pivot. Great teamwork!',end:true}}),
tip('Practice with a partner who pretends one side is weak. Ask them which side felt safest to stand on.')]],
["Walking with a patient and if a patient starts to fall",["After a transfer, a patient may need help to **walk** (ambulate). Use a **gait belt**, stand slightly **behind and to the weak side**, and hold the belt from behind with one hand. Walk at the patient’s pace, watch for dizziness and make sure the path is clear and the floor is dry. Walkers and canes are used as the therapist taught—a cane is held on the **strong side**.","If a patient starts to fall, **do not try to stop the fall**—you could both get hurt. Keep your back straight, widen your stance, pull the patient close using the gait belt and **slide them gently down your leg to the floor**, protecting their head. Stay with the patient, **call for help** and do not move them until the nurse checks them. Report every fall, even when the patient says they feel fine.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"If a patient starts to fall","steps":["Widen your stance and keep your back straight","Pull the patient close with the gait belt","Slide the patient gently down your leg to the floor","Protect the head","Stay with the patient and call for help","Do not move them until the nurse checks them"]},{"t":"check","q":"Which side does a patient hold a cane on?","options":["The strong side","The weak side","Either side","Behind the back"],"why":"The cane supports the weak side when held on the strong side."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"What judges look for","text":"Judges watch the wheelchair position and the brakes first—the chair must be on the strong side, and both the wheelchair and the bed must be locked. They check the gait belt placement and tightness, your foot position, your knee brace and whether you give a clear signal, such as counting to three. They watch for small steps and a full pivot so the patient feels the seat with the backs of the legs before sitting. Lifting the patient under the arms or twisting your back costs points and is unsafe. Finish by removing the belt, covering the patient and checking comfort."}]]
],takeaways:['Body mechanics: wide base, bend your knees, keep the patient close, turn with your feet; the strong side leads.','Set up: chair on the strong side, wheelchair and bed locked, bed low, head raised, rail down, linens fan-folded; dangle and watch for stress; robe and non-slip slippers.','Gait belt over clothes, buckle in front, fingers fit; feet 12 inches apart, brace knees, count to three, lift on the belt, small steps, pivot, hands on armrests and sit; cover and check comfort.'],terms:[['Gait belt','A strong belt around the patient’s waist that gives the caregiver a safe handhold.'],['Dangling','Sitting on the edge of the bed with the feet hanging down before standing.'],['Body mechanics','Using the body safely to lift and move.']],mcqs:[q('Where do you place the wheelchair for a patient with a weak side?','On the strong side','On the weak side','Behind the bed','In the hallway','The strong side leads.'),q('What do you lock before the transfer?','The wheelchair and the bed wheels','Only the door','Nothing','The window','Locked wheels prevent sliding.'),q('Where does the gait belt go?','Around the waist over clothing','Around the chest','Around the neck','Under the clothing on bare skin','Buckle in front.'),q('How far apart are your feet during the transfer?','About 12 inches','Touching','3 feet','It does not matter','A wide, steady base.'),q('What is a sign of stress during dangling?','Dizziness','Smiling','Talking about the weather','Asking for slippers','Help them lie back and tell the nurse.'),q('What does the patient do with their hands to stand?','Push against the bed','Hold your neck','Hold the gait belt buckle','Wave','Pushing helps them stand.'),q('Before sitting down, the patient should:','Feel the seat with the backs of the legs and hold the armrests','Close their eyes','Turn around quickly','Jump','Then bend the knees.'),q('How long do you have for Skill VI?','6 minutes','10 minutes','3 minutes','12 minutes','Practice with a timer.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to transfer a patient with a weak side from bed to a wheelchair following HOSA Skill VI.',rubric:[['Setting up',['strong side','lock','footrest','lower','head of bed','side rail','fan']],['Dangling and belt',['dangle','stress','dizzy','slippers','gait belt','waist','buckle','fingers']],['Transfer',['12 inches','knee','push','count','lift','small steps','pivot','armrests','sit']],['Finishing',['footrest','remove','blanket','comfortable','hand','communication']]],model:'I gather the wheelchair, gait belt, robe, non-slip slippers and blanket, wash my hands, greet and identify the patient, explain the skill and provide privacy. I place the wheelchair at the head of the bed facing the foot on the patient’s strong side, lock its wheels and fold up the footrests. I lower and lock the bed, raise the head of the bed, lower the exit-side rail and fan-fold the linens. I help the patient dangle and watch for stress such as dizziness or pale skin, then put on their robe and non-slip slippers. I apply the gait belt around the waist over the clothing with the buckle in front, snug enough that my fingers fit under it. I stand in front of the patient with my feet about 12 inches apart, brace their knee with mine and ask them to push on the bed on the count of three while I lift up on the belt. The patient takes small steps and pivots until the backs of their legs touch the seat, places their hands on the armrests, bends their knees and sits. I adjust the footrests, remove the gait belt, cover them with a blanket, check that they are comfortable and wash my hands.'},sources:[RULES,NIOSH_LIFT,CDC_STEADI,AHRQ_FALLS]};

export const C8={title:'Temperature, pulse and respirations (Skill VII, part 1)',hook:'Vital signs are the body’s “signs of life.” They tell the nurse how a patient is doing. Skill VII is the biggest skill in Personal Care: 12 minutes for temperature, pulse, respirations and blood pressure. This lesson covers the first three—TPR.',objectives:['Name the four vital signs and their usual adult ranges.','Take an oral temperature with an electronic thermometer following Skill VII.','Count a radial pulse and respirations for a full minute.','Describe pulse and breathing quality and record them.'],sections:[
['What vital signs tell us',[
'The four main vital signs are **temperature (T)**, **pulse (P)**, **respirations (R)** and **blood pressure (BP)**. Many facilities also check **pain** and **oxygen level**. Vital signs can show a problem, such as a fever or infection, before the patient feels very sick. That is why caregivers must measure **accurately** and **report anything unusual** to the nurse right away.',
'Usual adult teaching ranges are: oral temperature about **97.6–99.6 °F**, pulse **60–100 beats per minute**, respirations **12–20 breaths per minute** and blood pressure **below 120/80 mm Hg**. Everyone is a little different, so the nurse compares each reading with the patient’s usual numbers.',
{t:'table',caption:'Usual adult teaching ranges',head:['Vital sign','Usual range','Unit'],rows:[['Oral temperature','About 97.6–99.6','°F'],['Pulse','60–100','beats per minute'],['Respirations','12–20','breaths per minute'],['Blood pressure','Below 120/80','mm Hg']]},
{t:'w',kind:'vitalsreport',title:'Report or record?',prompt:'Decide which readings fall within the usual adult teaching ranges and which you would report to the nurse.',person:'patient',reportTo:'nurse'}]],
['Getting ready: steps 1–7',[
'**1.** Gather equipment: electronic thermometer and probe covers, a watch with a second hand, a stethoscope, a blood pressure cuff, alcohol wipes and the **graphic form** for recording. **2.** Wash your hands. **3.** Greet the patient and introduce yourself. **4.** Identify the patient. **5.** Explain the skill.',
'**6.** **Ask the patient whether they have eaten, had something to drink or smoked** recently. Hot or cold drinks, food and smoking can change an oral temperature, so you would wait about 15–20 minutes. **7.** Position the patient comfortably, usually sitting or lying with the arm supported.',
chk('Why ask about eating, drinking and smoking?',['They can change an oral temperature reading','It is polite conversation','To plan the patient’s meals','It changes blood type'],'Wait about 15–20 minutes if they recently ate, drank or smoked.')]],
['Taking a temperature: step 8',[
'**8a.** Put a **disposable sheath or probe cover** on the thermometer. **8b.** Ask the patient to **open their mouth and lift their tongue**. Slowly put the probe in until the tip touches the **base of the mouth under the tongue, to one side** (this area is called the sublingual pocket). **8c.** Ask the patient to **close their mouth**. **8d.** **Hold the probe in place until it beeps**.',
'**8e.** Take out the probe. **8f.** **Read the temperature and say it out loud** to the judge. **8g.** Throw the cover into the **biohazard container without touching it**—most thermometers have an eject button. **8h.** Put the probe back in its holder. **8i.** **Record** the temperature on the **graphic form**.',
order('Oral temperature',['Put on a probe cover','Patient opens mouth and lifts tongue','Place the tip under the tongue to one side','Patient closes mouth; hold until it beeps','Remove, read and say the temperature','Eject the cover into biohazard without touching it','Return the probe and record'])]],
['Counting the radial pulse: step 9',[
'The **pulse** is the heartbeat you can feel in an artery. **9a.** Support the patient’s **hand and arm** so they rest comfortably. **9b.** Find the **radial artery** by placing your **index and middle fingers** on the inside of the wrist on the **thumb side**. Never use your thumb—it has its own pulse. **9c.** Press **lightly** and count for **one full minute**.',
'While you count, notice the pulse **quality**. **Volume or strength**: is it strong, weak or bounding? **Rhythm**: is it **regular** (even) or **irregular** (uneven)? You will describe these to the judge.',
match('Pulse words',[['Regular','Beats come at even times'],['Irregular','Beats come at uneven times'],['Strong','Easy to feel'],['Weak or thready','Hard to feel'],['Bounding','Very strong, pounding'],['Radial site','Thumb side of the wrist']])]],
['Counting respirations: steps 10–13',[
'**10a.** After counting the pulse, **keep your fingers on the wrist**. This way the patient does not know you are counting breaths—people often change their breathing when they know someone is watching. **10b.** Count **respirations for one full minute**. One rise and one fall of the chest is **one breath**.',
'**11.** Say the pulse and respiration counts out loud. They must be **within 2** of the judge’s count. **12.** Describe the **pulse quality** (volume and rhythm) and the **respiration quality** (**depth**—deep, normal or shallow—and **rhythm**—regular or irregular). **13.** **Record** the pulse and respirations on the graphic form.',
flip('Breathing words',[['One breath','One rise and one fall of the chest'],['Shallow','Small chest movements'],['Deep','Large chest movements'],['Regular rhythm','Even timing'],['Labored','Hard work to breathe—report it'],['Within 2','Your count must match the judge within 2']])]],
['Practice and report',[
{t:'example',title:'Is this within range?',prompt:'An adult patient has T 98.4 °F, P 112 beats per minute (regular, strong) and R 18 breaths per minute (regular, normal depth). What should you do? (Values are invented for practice.)',steps:['Temperature 98.4 °F is in the usual range.','Respirations 18 are in the usual range (12–20).','Pulse 112 is above 100.'],answer:'Record all three readings and report the high pulse to the nurse.'},
kase('Accuracy check',{a:{text:'You count the pulse for 15 seconds and get 18. What does the rating sheet ask for?',choices:[['Count for one full minute','b','Correct. Skill VII asks for a full minute.',true],['Multiply by 4 and move on','b','In the event, count a full minute.',false]]},b:{text:'You finish the pulse. How do you count respirations?',choices:[['Keep your fingers on the wrist and watch the chest for a full minute','c','Correct. The patient will not change their breathing.',true],['Tell the patient, “Breathe normally while I count”','c','Then they may change their breathing.',false]]},c:{text:'You say the counts, describe the quality and record them on the graphic form. Next: blood pressure!',end:true}})]],
["Other temperature and pulse sites",["Temperature can be taken in several places. **Oral** (mouth) is used in Skill VII. **Tympanic** (ear) and **temporal** (forehead) thermometers are quick. **Axillary** (armpit) readings are about 1 °F lower than oral, and **rectal** readings are about 1 °F higher and are used only when ordered. Do not take an oral temperature on a patient who is confused, unconscious, on oxygen by mask or who has had mouth surgery. Always write down **which site** you used.","The pulse can be felt at other arteries too: **carotid** (neck, used in emergencies), **brachial** (inside the elbow, used for blood pressure and for babies), **femoral** (groin) and **pedal** (top of the foot). An **apical** pulse is counted by listening over the heart with a stethoscope for a full minute. In Skill VII you use the **radial** pulse at the wrist.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the site","pairs":[["Oral","Under the tongue"],["Tympanic","In the ear"],["Temporal","Across the forehead"],["Axillary","In the armpit; reads lower"],["Carotid","Pulse in the neck"],["Apical","Heartbeat heard with a stethoscope"]]},{"t":"check","q":"Which patient should NOT have an oral temperature?","options":["A confused patient who might bite the probe","A patient who is awake and calm","A patient sitting up in bed","A patient who has not eaten for an hour"],"why":"Choose another site and record it."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"What judges look for","text":"Judges compare your pulse and respiration counts with their own; you must be within 2. They check that you asked about eating, drinking and smoking, used a probe cover, placed the probe correctly, read the temperature out loud and threw the cover away without touching it. They listen for you to describe pulse volume and rhythm and breathing depth and rhythm. Counting a full minute matters. Write numbers neatly on the graphic form—a reading that is correct but hard to read can still cost points. Practice on many classmates so different pulses feel familiar."}]]
],takeaways:['Vital signs: temperature, pulse, respirations and blood pressure; know the usual adult ranges and report unusual readings.','Ask about eating, drinking and smoking; probe cover, under the tongue to one side, hold until it beeps, read aloud, eject without touching and record.','Radial pulse with two fingers on the thumb side for one full minute; keep fingers there and count breaths for a full minute; within ±2; describe quality and record.'],terms:[['Vital signs','Measurements of temperature, pulse, respirations and blood pressure.'],['Radial artery','The artery on the thumb side of the wrist.'],['Graphic form','The chart where vital signs are recorded.']],mcqs:[q('What is the usual adult pulse range?','60–100 beats per minute','12–20 beats per minute','100–150 beats per minute','20–40 beats per minute','12–20 is for respirations.'),q('What is the usual adult respiration range?','12–20 breaths per minute','60–100','30–40','4–8','Count for a full minute.'),q('Where do you place the oral thermometer probe?','Under the tongue to one side','On top of the tongue','Between the teeth','Under the lip','The sublingual pocket.'),q('Which fingers do you use to take a radial pulse?','Index and middle fingers','Thumb','Pinky','Whole hand','The thumb has its own pulse.'),q('How long do you count the pulse in Skill VII?','One full minute','10 seconds','15 seconds','5 minutes','Full minute for accuracy.'),q('Why keep your fingers on the wrist while counting breaths?','So the patient does not change their breathing','To keep the patient warm','To check the temperature','It is not needed','People breathe differently when watched.'),q('How close must your counts be to the judge’s?','Within 2','Within 10','Exactly the same','Within 20','±2 for pulse and respirations.'),q('How do you throw away the probe cover?','Eject it into the biohazard container without touching it','Pull it off with your fingers','Leave it on','Put it in your pocket','Avoid touching saliva.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to measure temperature, pulse and respirations following HOSA Skill VII, and give the usual adult ranges.',rubric:[['Preparation',['identify','explain','eating','drinking','smoking','position']],['Temperature',['probe cover','tongue','one side','close','beep','read','biohazard','record']],['Pulse',['radial','thumb side','index','middle','full minute','volume','rhythm']],['Respirations and ranges',['fingers','unaware','full minute','depth','rhythm','within 2','60','12']]],model:'I gather the thermometer, probe covers, a watch with a second hand and the graphic form, wash my hands, greet and identify the patient and explain the skill. I ask whether the patient has eaten, had something to drink or smoked, because that can change an oral temperature, and I position them comfortably. I put a probe cover on, ask the patient to open and lift their tongue, place the tip under the tongue to one side, have them close their mouth and hold it until it beeps. I read the temperature aloud, eject the cover into the biohazard container without touching it, return the probe and record the result. I support the arm and place my index and middle fingers on the thumb side of the wrist over the radial artery, press lightly and count for one full minute, noting whether the pulse is strong or weak and regular or irregular. I keep my fingers there so the patient is unaware and count breaths for a full minute, noting depth and rhythm. My counts should be within 2 of the judge’s. I record them and report readings outside the usual adult ranges: temperature about 97.6–99.6 °F, pulse 60–100 and respirations 12–20.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_VITALS,MEDLINE_PULSE,MEDLINE_TEMP]};

export const C9={title:'Measuring blood pressure with an aneroid cuff (Skill VII, part 2)',hook:'Blood pressure is the push of blood against the walls of the arteries. To measure it, you squeeze the arm with a cuff and listen with a stethoscope. This lesson finishes Skill VII. Your readings must be within 2 mm Hg of the judge’s—so practice a lot!',objectives:['Explain systolic and diastolic pressure.','Place the cuff and stethoscope correctly.','Inflate to 180 mm Hg and deflate at 2–4 mm Hg per second.','Record the reading and complete the Skill VII checkpoints.'],sections:[
['What blood pressure means',[
'Every time the heart beats, it pushes blood into the arteries. The **systolic** pressure is the **top number**: the pressure when the heart **squeezes**. The **diastolic** pressure is the **bottom number**: the pressure when the heart **rests** between beats. Blood pressure is written like a fraction, such as **118/76**, and measured in **millimeters of mercury (mm Hg)**.',
'High blood pressure (hypertension) can cause strokes and heart attacks, often without symptoms. Low blood pressure can cause dizziness and falls. The American Heart Association calls a reading **below 120/80** normal for adults. Always report readings that are much higher or lower than the patient’s usual numbers.',
label('heart','The heart pumps blood into the arteries',[['lv','Left ventricle','Squeezes to push blood into the body (systolic).'],['aorta','Aorta','The largest artery; carries blood from the heart.'],['la','Left atrium','Receives blood from the lungs.'],['ra','Right atrium','Receives blood from the body.'],['rv','Right ventricle','Pumps blood to the lungs.']])]],
['Equipment and getting ready: steps 14a–14f',[
'An **aneroid** blood pressure set has a **cuff**, a **bulb** with a **valve**, and a round **gauge** with a needle. A **stethoscope** lets you hear the sounds. **14a.** **Clean the earpieces and diaphragm** of the stethoscope with an alcohol wipe. **14b.** Uncover the patient’s **upper arm** and rest it **level with the heart**, **palm up**.',
'**14c.** With the **valve open**, squeeze the cuff to push out **all the air**. **14d.** Use your fingertips to find the **brachial artery** on the inside of the elbow. **14e.** Wrap the cuff **snugly and smoothly** around the arm about **1 inch above the elbow**. **14f.** Put the **center of the cuff** (often marked with an arrow) **over the brachial artery**.',
order('Prepare the cuff',['Clean the stethoscope earpieces and diaphragm','Arm level with the heart, palm up','Open the valve and squeeze out all the air','Find the brachial artery','Wrap the cuff snugly 1 inch above the elbow','Center the cuff over the brachial artery'])]],
['Taking the reading: steps 14g–14m',[
'**14g.** **Close the valve** by turning it **clockwise**. **14h.** Put the stethoscope **earpieces in your ears**, pointing **forward**. **14i.** **Feel the brachial artery** again. **14j.** Place the **stethoscope diaphragm over the brachial artery**. **14k.** **Inflate the cuff to 180 mm Hg**.',
'**14l.** Open the valve by turning it **counterclockwise** so the needle falls **slowly and evenly**, about **2–4 mm Hg per second**. **14m.** Listen. The **first clear sound** you hear is the **systolic** pressure. Keep deflating. The **last sound** you hear is the **diastolic** pressure. If you deflate too fast, you will miss the sounds and the reading will be wrong.',
match('Valve and sounds',[['Clockwise','Closes the valve'],['Counterclockwise','Opens the valve'],['180 mm Hg','How high to inflate'],['2–4 mm Hg per second','How fast to deflate'],['First sound','Systolic (top number)'],['Last sound','Diastolic (bottom number)']])]],
['Finishing the blood pressure: steps 14n–14s',[
'**14n.** **Deflate the cuff completely**, take it off and make the patient comfortable. **14o.** **Record** the blood pressure on the graphic form, such as **126/82**. **14p.** Clean the stethoscope earpieces again. **14q.** Put the equipment back where it belongs. **14r–s.** Your systolic and diastolic readings must each be **within 2 mm Hg** of the judge’s.',
'Gauges are marked every **2 mm Hg**, so blood pressure is recorded in **even numbers**. Read the needle at **eye level**. If you are not sure of a reading, completely deflate the cuff, wait about a minute and try again.',
chk('A gauge needle stops between 124 and 126 at the first sound. How should you think about it?',['Read the nearest even line at eye level, and recheck after waiting if you are unsure','Write 125','Guess 130','Skip recording'],'Aneroid gauges have 2 mm Hg marks, so readings are even numbers.')]],
['Completing Skill VII: steps 15–19',[
'**15.** Wash your hands. **16.** Do all the vital signs **smoothly and in a logical order**, **overlapping** when you can. For example, while the thermometer is in the patient’s mouth, you can start counting the pulse. **17.** Do the **checkpoints**: call light within easy reach, bed lowered, patient in good body alignment and report any changes to the nurse.',
'**18.** Use kind words and body language. **19.** Use standard precautions. Skill VII is worth the most points in Personal Care (81). Because ties are broken by the highest-point skill, doing Skill VII well really matters.',
flip('Skill VII checkpoints',[['Overlap','Count the pulse while the thermometer is in'],['Call light','Within easy reach'],['Bed','Lowered'],['Alignment','Patient positioned straight and comfortable'],['Report','Tell the nurse about any changes'],['Points','Skill VII is worth 81 points—the most']])]],
['Practice scenarios',[
{t:'example',title:'Read and decide',prompt:'You hear the first sound at 148 and the last sound at 92. The patient’s usual reading is 120/78. What do you record and do? (Values are invented for practice.)',steps:['First sound = systolic = 148.','Last sound = diastolic = 92.','Both are higher than the patient’s usual reading and above 120/80.'],answer:'Record 148/92 on the graphic form and report it to the nurse.'},
kase('Blood pressure troubleshooting',{a:{text:'You inflate the cuff, but the needle keeps falling quickly. What is wrong?',choices:[['The valve was not closed (turn it clockwise)','b','Correct. Close the valve before inflating.',true],['The patient is moving','b','Check the valve first.',false]]},b:{text:'You let the air out very fast and did not hear the first sound. What now?',choices:[['Deflate completely, wait about a minute and measure again slowly','c','Correct. Deflate at 2–4 mm Hg per second.',true],['Guess the number','c','Never guess a vital sign.',false]]},c:{text:'You measure again, hear clear sounds and record an even-number reading. Accurate work!',end:true}})]],
["Avoiding common blood pressure mistakes",["Small mistakes change the reading. A **cuff that is too small** gives a reading that is **too high**; a cuff that is **too large** gives a reading that is **too low**. An arm that hangs **below the heart** reads high. Talking, crossed legs, a full bladder, recent exercise, caffeine or smoking can also raise the number. Have the patient sit or lie quietly, with feet flat and the arm supported.","Other errors come from the caregiver: deflating too fast, not hearing well because the earpieces point backward, placing the cuff over clothing, or reading the gauge from an angle. Do not measure blood pressure on an arm with an **IV**, a **dialysis access**, a **cast** or on the side of a **mastectomy**, and follow any “no blood pressure” sign over the bed. If you are unsure, deflate fully, wait about a minute and recheck.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Will the reading be too high or too low?","buckets":["Too high","Too low"],"items":[["Cuff too small",0],["Cuff too large",1],["Arm hanging below the heart",0],["Patient talking during the reading",0],["Patient just drank coffee",0]]},{"t":"check","q":"Where should you NOT take a blood pressure?","options":["On an arm with an IV","On a bare upper arm level with the heart","On the arm the nurse chose","On a relaxed, supported arm"],"why":"Also avoid arms with a dialysis access, cast or mastectomy side."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"What judges look for","text":"Judges check your systolic and diastolic readings against theirs; each must be within 2 mm Hg. They watch that you cleaned the stethoscope before and after, positioned the arm level with the heart, fully deflated the cuff, placed it 1 inch above the elbow and centered it over the brachial artery. They check that you inflate to 180 mm Hg and deflate slowly. They also score whether you overlapped the vital signs to save time and completed the checkpoints. Practice with a double-headed teaching stethoscope so a teacher can hear the same sounds you hear."}]]
],takeaways:['Systolic is the first sound (heart squeezing); diastolic is the last sound (heart resting); adult normal is below 120/80 mm Hg.','Clean the stethoscope, arm level with the heart palm up, deflate the cuff, find the brachial artery, cuff snug 1 inch above the elbow centered over the artery.','Close the valve clockwise, inflate to 180, deflate counterclockwise at 2–4 mm Hg per second, record even numbers within ±2 and complete the checkpoints.'],terms:[['Systolic','The top number: pressure when the heart squeezes.'],['Diastolic','The bottom number: pressure when the heart rests.'],['Brachial artery','The artery on the inside of the elbow used for blood pressure.']],mcqs:[q('What is the systolic pressure?','The first sound you hear','The last sound you hear','The pulse rate','The temperature','It is the top number.'),q('What is the diastolic pressure?','The last sound you hear','The first sound you hear','The highest number on the gauge','180 mm Hg','It is the bottom number.'),q('How high do you inflate the cuff in Skill VII?','180 mm Hg','100 mm Hg','300 mm Hg','60 mm Hg','Per the rating sheet.'),q('How fast do you let the air out?','2–4 mm Hg per second','20 mm Hg per second','All at once','1 mm Hg per minute','Slow and even.'),q('Which way do you turn the valve to close it?','Clockwise','Counterclockwise','Up','It has no valve','Counterclockwise opens it.'),q('Where is the cuff placed?','About 1 inch above the elbow','On the wrist','Over the shoulder','Below the elbow','Centered over the brachial artery.'),q('How should the arm be positioned?','Level with the heart, palm up','Hanging down','Above the head','Palm down on the lap','For an accurate reading.'),q('How close must your blood pressure be to the judge’s?','Within 2 mm Hg','Within 10 mm Hg','Within 20 mm Hg','Exactly the same','For both systolic and diastolic.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to measure blood pressure with an aneroid cuff following HOSA Skill VII, including how to identify the systolic and diastolic pressures.',rubric:[['Preparation',['clean','earpieces','arm','level','heart','palm','deflate','brachial']],['Cuff placement',['snug','1 inch','elbow','center','artery']],['Measurement',['clockwise','180','counterclockwise','2-4','first sound','systolic','last sound','diastolic']],['Finishing',['remove','record','clean','within 2','checkpoint','call light','report']]],model:'I clean the stethoscope earpieces and diaphragm, expose the patient’s upper arm and rest it level with the heart with the palm up. With the valve open, I squeeze all the air out of the cuff, find the brachial artery with my fingertips and wrap the cuff snugly and smoothly about 1 inch above the elbow with its center over the artery. I close the valve by turning it clockwise, place the earpieces in my ears, feel the brachial artery again, put the diaphragm over it and inflate the cuff to 180 mm Hg. I open the valve counterclockwise so the needle falls evenly at 2–4 mm Hg per second. The first clear sound is the systolic pressure, when the heart squeezes, and the last sound is the diastolic pressure, when the heart rests. I deflate the cuff completely, remove it, make the patient comfortable, record the reading in even numbers on the graphic form, clean the earpieces and put the equipment away. My readings should be within 2 mm Hg of the judge’s. I finish the checkpoints—call light within reach, bed lowered, good alignment—and report readings that are unusual for the patient to the nurse.'},sources:[RULES,AHA_BP,AHA_MEASURE,MEDLINE_VITALS]};

export const C10={title:'Ostomy care (Skill VIII)',hook:'Some people have surgery that makes a new opening on the belly so stool can leave the body. This opening is called a stoma, and stool collects in a pouch. Changing the pouch with care and respect is Skill VIII. It has 36 steps and 10 minutes.',objectives:['Explain what an ostomy and a stoma are.','Prepare the patient with privacy and protection.','Remove, clean and replace an ostomy pouch following HOSA Skill VIII.','Observe, document and report stool and stoma changes.'],sections:[
['What is an ostomy?',[
'An **ostomy** is a surgery that makes an opening from an organ to the outside of the body. A **colostomy** connects the **large intestine (colon)** to the belly. An **ileostomy** connects the **small intestine** to the belly. The opening is the **stoma**. A healthy stoma is **pink or red and moist**, like the inside of your mouth. It has **no feeling**, so it does not hurt to touch gently.',
'Stool cannot be held back, so it collects in an **ostomy pouch** (bag) stuck to the skin around the stoma. People have ostomies because of cancer, injury or diseases such as Crohn’s disease. Many people with ostomies live active lives. They may feel embarrassed, so caregivers stay **calm, kind and respectful**—never make faces at the smell or sight.',
label('gi','Where ostomies come from',[['smallint','Small intestine','An ileostomy comes from here; stool is more liquid.'],['largeint','Large intestine (colon)','A colostomy comes from here; stool is more formed.'],['rectum','Rectum','Normally stores stool before it leaves the body.'],['stomach','Stomach','Mixes food with acid.'],['esophagus','Esophagus','Carries food to the stomach.']])]],
['Getting ready: steps 1–11',[
'**1.** Gather supplies: clean pouch and clamp, gloves, two protective pads, a bath blanket, wipes, a washcloth, warm water, a towel, a plastic bag and deodorant if ordered. **2.** Wash your hands. **3.** **Knock** before entering. **4.** Greet the patient and introduce yourself. **5.** Identify the patient. **6.** Explain the skill. **7.** Give **privacy**.',
'**8.** Raise the bed to **hip level** and **lock the wheels**. **9.** Lower the side rail as needed. **10.** Wash your hands again. **11.** Put on **non-latex gloves**. Stool contains germs, so gloves are always needed for this skill.',
order('Before you touch the pouch',['Gather supplies and wash hands','Knock, greet and introduce yourself','Identify the patient and explain','Provide privacy','Bed at hip level, wheels locked','Lower the side rail as needed','Wash hands again and put on gloves'])]],
['Protecting the patient: steps 12–15',[
'**12.** Help the patient lie on their **back** if possible and put a **protective pad under the buttocks**. **13.** Cover the patient with a **bath blanket**. **14.** **Fan-fold the top linens** down and move clothing to **show only the stoma area**. **15.** Put a **second protective pad beside the patient’s body** and **cover the patient from the waist down**.',
'These steps keep the bed clean and the patient **warm and covered**. Showing only the area you are caring for protects the patient’s **dignity**.',
chk('Why do you place a second protective pad beside the patient?',['To protect the bed and catch any spills','To keep the patient cool','To hold the clean pouch','It is not needed'],'The pad protects linens near the stoma.')]],
['Removing and cleaning: steps 16–21',[
'**16.** **Pull gently on one edge of the pouch to let out air**. **17.** **Carefully remove the pouch**, peeling it away from the skin while gently supporting the skin, and put it in a **plastic bag**. **18.** Wipe around the stoma with a **disposable wipe** and throw it away.',
'**19.** Wash with a **washcloth and warm water**, **moving in one direction, away from the stoma**, so germs are not pushed toward it. **20.** **Rinse and pat dry** with a towel. Cover the stoma opening for a moment with a **wipe** to catch any stool. **21.** Apply **deodorant** if the scenario says to, and throw away the wipe.',
order('Remove and clean',['Pull one edge to release air','Peel the pouch off carefully into a plastic bag','Wipe around the stoma and discard','Wash with warm water away from the stoma','Rinse and pat dry','Cover the stoma briefly with a wipe','Apply deodorant if ordered'])]],
['New pouch and clean-up: steps 22–34',[
'**22.** Put on the **clean pouch**: hold it in place and **press to seal**, making sure the **bottom is clamped** so stool cannot leak. **23.** Remove the bed protector and throw it away. **24.** Put soiled linens in the proper container. **25.** Throw away the plastic bag properly. **26.** Remove your gloves the right way and throw them away. **27.** Wash your hands. **28.** Make the patient comfortable. **29.** Lower the bed and **open the privacy curtain**. **30.** Leave the **call light within reach**. **31.** Wash your hands.',
'**32.** **Document** the skill. **33.** Write down the stool’s **color, odor, consistency and amount**. **34.** **Report** any changes in the **stoma** or the patient to the nurse. A stoma that is **pale, dark red, purple or black**, swollen or bleeding, red or broken skin around it, or no stool for a long time must be reported right away.',
sort('Expected or report?',['Usually expected','Report to the nurse'],[['Pink, moist stoma',0],['Purple or black stoma',1],['Skin around the stoma red and broken',1],['Stoma bleeding a lot',1],['Formed brown stool from a colostomy',0],['Pouch sealed and clamped',0]]),
{t:'w',kind:'flip',title:'What to document',cards:[['Color','Brown, black, red, green…'],['Odor','Usual or unusually strong'],['Consistency','Liquid, soft or formed'],['Amount','Small, medium or large'],['Stoma','Pink and moist, or a change to report'],['Skin','Healthy or red, broken or irritated']]}]],
['Respect and practice',[
'Ostomy care is personal. Speak kindly, keep the patient covered and let them help if they want to—many people know how to care for their own ostomy. Use the steps that appear in every skill: **knock, greet, identify, explain, privacy, gloves, hand hygiene, call light, report and document**.',
kase('Ostomy care decisions',{a:{text:'You uncover the stoma and see that it is pink and moist. What does that mean?',choices:[['It looks healthy','b','Correct. A healthy stoma is pink or red and moist.',true],['It is infected','b','Pink and moist is the healthy look.',false]]},b:{text:'You are washing the skin. Which direction do you wash?',choices:[['In one direction, away from the stoma','c','Correct. This keeps germs away from the stoma.',true],['Back and forth over the stoma','c','Wash away from the stoma.',false]]},c:{text:'You seal and clamp the new pouch, clean up, wash your hands and document the stool’s color, odor, consistency and amount. Excellent!',end:true}})]],
["Living with an ostomy and emptying the pouch",["Between changes, the pouch is **emptied** when it is about **one-third to one-half full**, so it does not pull off the skin or leak. Wear gloves, put a protective pad down, unclamp the bottom over a bedpan or toilet, let the stool drain, clean the end of the pouch, reclamp it and measure or describe the output. A full pouch change (Skill VIII) is usually done every few days, or sooner if it leaks.","Patients with ostomies may worry about **odor, leaks and how they look**. Caregivers help by keeping the pouch clean and sealed, using deodorant if ordered, and treating the patient normally. A dietitian may suggest chewing food well and drinking enough water, especially with an **ileostomy**, which loses more fluid. Report signs of **dehydration** such as dry mouth, dark urine or dizziness. The United Ostomy Associations of America offers support groups for patients and families.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Emptying an ostomy pouch","steps":["Wash hands and put on gloves","Place a protective pad","Unclamp the bottom over a bedpan or toilet","Let the stool drain","Clean the end of the pouch","Reclamp and describe the output"]},{"t":"check","q":"When should an ostomy pouch be emptied?","options":["When it is about one-third to one-half full","Only when it is completely full","Once a week","Never; it is only changed"],"why":"An overfull pouch can pull away and leak."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"What judges look for","text":"Judges watch for privacy and dignity throughout Skill VIII: knocking, closing the curtain, covering the patient with a bath blanket and exposing only the stoma. They check that you release the air before removing the pouch, wash away from the stoma, pat the skin dry and clamp the new pouch. They also check that every dirty item goes in the right container and that you remove your gloves correctly. At the end, they look for documentation of the stool’s color, odor, consistency and amount and a report to the nurse about the stoma."}]]
],takeaways:['A stoma is an opening from the intestine to the belly (colostomy from the colon, ileostomy from the small intestine); a healthy stoma is pink and moist.','Knock, greet, identify, explain, privacy; hip-level locked bed; gloves; pads and a bath blanket; expose only the stoma; release air, remove into a plastic bag, wash away from the stoma, pat dry.','Seal and clamp the new pouch, clean up, lowest bed, call light; document color, odor, consistency and amount and report stoma changes.'],terms:[['Ostomy','A surgical opening from an organ to the outside of the body.'],['Stoma','The opening on the belly where stool leaves the body.'],['Colostomy','An ostomy made from the large intestine (colon).']],mcqs:[q('What does a healthy stoma look like?','Pink or red and moist','Black and dry','White and pale','Purple','Report other colors.'),q('What is the first thing you do to the old pouch?','Pull gently on one edge to release air','Rip it off quickly','Empty it on the bed','Cut it','Then remove it carefully.'),q('Where does the used pouch go?','In a plastic bag','On the floor','In the sink','Back on the patient','Then discard properly.'),q('Which direction do you wash around the stoma?','In one direction, away from the stoma','Toward the stoma','In circles over the stoma','You do not wash it','Keeps germs away from the stoma.'),q('What must the bottom of the new pouch be?','Clamped','Open','Cut','Folded up without a clamp','Prevents leaks.'),q('Which should you document about the stool?','Color, odor, consistency and amount','Only the color','Nothing','The patient’s favorite food','All four.'),q('A colostomy comes from which organ?','Large intestine','Small intestine','Stomach','Liver','An ileostomy comes from the small intestine.'),q('Which stoma change must you report?','A purple or black stoma','A pink stoma','A moist stoma','A stoma with a sealed pouch','Color changes can mean poor blood flow.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to give ostomy care following HOSA Skill VIII, including what you observe and report.',rubric:[['Preparation and privacy',['knock','identify','explain','privacy','hip level','lock','gloves','pad','bath blanket']],['Removing and cleaning',['air','remove','plastic bag','wipe','warm water','away from the stoma','pat dry']],['New pouch and clean-up',['clean pouch','seal','clamp','discard','gloves','lowest','call light']],['Observe and report',['color','odor','consistency','amount','stoma','report','document']]],model:'I gather supplies, wash my hands, knock, greet the patient, introduce myself, identify them, explain the skill and provide privacy. I raise the bed to hip level, lock the wheels, lower the side rail as needed, wash my hands again and put on non-latex gloves. I help the patient lie on their back, place a protective pad under the buttocks, cover them with a bath blanket, fan-fold the linens to expose only the stoma, and place a second pad alongside them while covering them from the waist down. I pull gently on one edge of the pouch to release air, remove it carefully into a plastic bag and wipe around the stoma. I wash with a washcloth and warm water in one direction away from the stoma, rinse and pat dry, cover the stoma briefly with a wipe and apply deodorant if ordered. I apply the clean pouch, hold it to secure the seal and make sure the bottom is clamped. I discard the bed protector, linens, plastic bag and gloves properly, wash my hands, make the patient comfortable, lower the bed, open the curtain and leave the call light within reach. I document the skill, including the stool’s color, odor, consistency and amount, and report any change to the stoma, such as a dark or pale color or irritated skin, to the nurse.'},sources:[RULES,UOAA,MEDLINE_OSTOMY]};
